# Local-dev configuration: copy to `.env` and adjust.
# Not needed for the playground: `pnpm play` sets the playground's values itself
# (a variable already set in your shell wins); `.env` is for `pnpm dev`.
#
# Loaded by `pnpm dev` via dotenv-cli (`dotenv -e .env`): plain KEY=VALUE
# lines, no `export`, no quotes around inline-JSON values. `pnpm dev` runs
# without a `.env`, but then with every module, analytics too, for which the
# playground runs no Prometheus (see MCP_ACTIVE_MODULES below).

# ── Camunda 7 / CIB seven engine (read by packages/connectors/engine/engine-connector/src/module.ts) ──
ENGINE_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8410/engine-rest
# Engine id for the single-engine shorthand above (default "default"). An
# engine with the metrics plugin stamps its own id as the `engine_id` label on
# every metric, and analytics reads only the configured engine ids: the two
# MUST match, or every analytics query for the engine returns nothing and its
# health reads `unknown`. The Compose stack's engine exports no metrics; the
# server knows it as `prod-a`.
ENGINE_ID=prod-a
# Engine vendor for the shorthand: camunda7 (default) | cibseven | operaton.
# It picks the cockpit-link routes and the product name the views show; the
# Compose stack's engine is CIB seven.
ENGINE_FLAVOR=cibseven
# basic | bearer | passthrough | none: passthrough forwards each MCP caller's
# bearer token to the engine per request (no static credentials; requires an
# engine with REST authentication enabled, a default engine ignores the token).
# basic requires USERNAME+PASSWORD, bearer requires TOKEN, enforced at boot.
ENGINE_AUTH_TYPE=basic
ENGINE_USERNAME=demo
ENGINE_PASSWORD=demo
# ENGINE_TOKEN=...                      # only for ENGINE_AUTH_TYPE=bearer
# Per-request deadline for every engine call, in milliseconds (default 30000):
# a hung engine fails the tool call with "engine <id> did not respond within
# … ms" instead of holding it for minutes. Whole milliseconds only; anything
# else ("30s") fails the boot.
# ENGINE_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# Multi-engine (overrides ENGINE_BASE_URL + ENGINE_ID). Each `id` is
# subject to the same ENGINE_ID match as above. The second engine on :8411 only
# runs under `docker compose --profile multi-engine up -d`. Each entry may
# carry its own `auth` ({type, username?, password?, token?}); entries without
# one fall back to the global ENGINE_* settings above.
# Optional per engine: "flavor" ("cibseven" | "operaton" | "camunda7", default "camunda7") selects
# the vendor's cockpit-link routes. Mixed fleets in one server are supported.
# Both Compose engines are CIB seven.
# ENGINES_JSON=[{"id":"prod-a","baseUrl":"http://localhost:8410/engine-rest","flavor":"cibseven"},{"id":"prod-b","baseUrl":"http://localhost:8411/engine-rest","flavor":"cibseven","auth":{"type":"basic","username":"demo","password":"demo"}}]
#
# Environment-map form (same var, JSON object instead of array): keys are
# environment ids, values are that environment's engine entries. Pickers and
# `engine_list_engines` then group the engines by environment.
# Engine ids stay globally unique (they remain the ENGINE_ID join key); the
# flat array form may equivalently carry "environment" per entry.
# ENGINES_JSON={"prod-eu":[{"id":"prod-a","baseUrl":"http://localhost:8410/engine-rest","flavor":"cibseven"}],"prod-us":[{"id":"prod-b","baseUrl":"http://localhost:8411/engine-rest","flavor":"cibseven"}]}
#
# Where a call without `engine` routes when several engines are configured
# (else the call fails with ENGINE_NOT_SELECTED listing the engines). It must
# name a configured engine id, or the boot fails.
# ENGINE_DEFAULT=prod-a

# ── HTTP edge ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HTTP port (default 8400) and bind address: `pnpm start` and the image
# listen on every interface (0.0.0.0), `pnpm dev` on 127.0.0.1. HOST=127.0.0.1
# keeps a server behind a proxy in the same host or pod off the network.
# PORT=8400
# HOST=0.0.0.0
# Public base URL when deployed behind a proxy, gateway or platform edge:
# advertised URLs, the origin of the OAuth token audience <MCP_URL>/mcp
# (required with OAuth, then an origin without a path), the Host/Origin the
# server accepts, and the origin the views load the Geist font from (/fonts/…
# on this server, declared in each view's CSP).
# `pnpm play --auth` sets http://localhost:<PORT>; `pnpm dev` sets nothing.
# MCP_URL=https://mcp.example.com
# DNS-rebinding protection: a request whose Host (or, on a POST, Origin) is
# not localhost-class, MCP_URL's or listed here gets 403. Set MCP_URL (or
# these comma lists) when the server is reached under any other name. `*`
# turns a check off (only behind an edge that validates Host itself).
# /health and /metrics stay reachable by IP for probes and scrapers.
# MCP_ALLOWED_HOSTS=mcp.internal.example.com
# MCP_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://app.example.com
# Request-body cap in bytes (default 4194304 = 4 MiB): larger bodies get 413
# before they are read; 4x the cap is held across in-flight requests at most
# (503 beyond). `pnpm dev` serves capped too (the same listener).
# MCP_MAX_BODY_BYTES=4194304
# Bearer token /metrics requires (unset = open, like the health probes).
# MCP_METRICS_TOKEN=change-me
# The request log on stdout: info (default, one line per request, no payloads;
# a failed request adds its error text), debug (adds tool arguments and
# results) or trace (full requests and responses, credential headers
# redacted). The boot warns at debug and trace.
# MCP_LOG_LEVEL=info

# ── Modules & toolsets ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MCP_ACTIVE_MODULES picks the modules (unset or "all" = every module), each
# with an optional `:<toolset>` suffix. Without a suffix the default fails
# CLOSED: read-only on this unauthenticated server (no OAuth), the engine module's
# operations toolset under OAuth. Analytics declares no toolsets (every tool
# is a read), so a suffix on it is ignored with a warning. `admin` is never
# implied; an empty (`engine:`) or unknown suffix warns and falls back to
# read-only. The boot log names the effective toolsets.
# The playground runs no Prometheus, so the first line below runs the engine
# module alone; add `analytics` once PROMETHEUS_URL names a Prometheus that
# holds your engines' metrics.
MCP_ACTIVE_MODULES=engine
# The line below widens the dev server to the admin surface (deployments also
# need ENGINE_ALLOW_DEPLOYMENTS below).
# Without OAuth it is open to ANYONE who reaches port 8400: `pnpm dev`
# binds 127.0.0.1 (unless HOST/--host says otherwise), but `pnpm start` and a
# plain `docker run -p 8400:8400` listen on every network interface (the
# compose stack publishes on 127.0.0.1 only). Keep it to trusted networks.
# MCP_ACTIVE_MODULES=engine:admin
# Registers engine_create_deployment (only together with engine:admin).
# Deploying a BPMN/DMN is CODE EXECUTION inside the engine JVM: JUEL
# expressions and scripts run with the engine's privileges. Strict: only
# "true" or "false" (empty = unset); any other value fails the boot.
# ENGINE_ALLOW_DEPLOYMENTS=true

# ── Prometheus (analytics data source) ────────────────────────────────────
# A Prometheus that holds your engines' metrics; the playground runs none.
# Unset → defaults to http://localhost:9090 (the bare Prometheus port), and
# the server warns at boot while analytics runs.
# PROMETHEUS_URL=http://localhost:9090
# Optional auth for a protected Prometheus (or Mimir/Cortex/Thanos/VictoriaMetrics
# behind an auth proxy): EITHER a bearer token OR basic auth; userinfo in
# PROMETHEUS_URL (https://user:pass@host) is moved into the basic header too.
# Ambiguous combinations fail the boot; credentials never appear in tool errors.
# PROMETHEUS_BEARER_TOKEN=...
# PROMETHEUS_USERNAME=grafana
# PROMETHEUS_PASSWORD=...
# Extra request headers as a JSON object, e.g. a multi-tenant id.
# PROMETHEUS_HEADERS={"X-Scope-OrgID":"tenant-a"}
# Per-query deadline in milliseconds (default 30000; whole milliseconds only).
# PROMETHEUS_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
# Analytics reads ONLY the configured engines (a Prometheus may be shared with
# other teams): with the engine module active, its engine ids. A standalone
# analytics boot (no engine module) names them here, comma-separated engine_id values;
# with neither, every analytics tool refuses.
# ANALYTICS_ENGINE_IDS=prod-a,prod-b

# ── OAuth (optional): one provider's variables ────────────────────────────
# Validates bearer tokens on /mcp against your IdP (requests without a valid
# token get 401) and serves the .well-known discovery metadata. Only the MCP
# endpoint is protected; widget assets and the dev inspector are not.
# Combine with ENGINE_AUTH_TYPE=passthrough to forward the validated token
# to the engine. It is also the ONLY caller identity (passthrough auth,
# per-caller caches; the server warns at boot without it). OAuth also raises
# the no-suffix toolset default from read-only to engine operations (admin
# still has to be named). Auth terminated in a gateway or reverse proxy IN
# FRONT of the server is invisible to it: such a setup boots read-only unless
# MCP_ACTIVE_MODULES names the toolsets (identity only ever comes from this
# OAuth, never a header).
# Set the variables of ONE provider. Tokens must carry <MCP_URL>/mcp in aud
# (RFC 8707) and a sub claim; the boot logs the audience it expects. What fails
# the boot: docs/mcp/operations.md#security, the identity provider's side:
# docs/mcp/operations.md#identity-provider-checklist.
# Keycloak (HTTPS, or HTTP on localhost only) and its realm:
# MCP_OAUTH_KEYCLOAK_SERVER_URL=https://kc.example.com
# MCP_OAUTH_KEYCLOAK_REALM=platform
# Auth0: the tenant domain or its issuer URL.
# MCP_OAUTH_AUTH0_DOMAIN=tenant.eu.auth0.com
# WorkOS AuthKit: its host.
# MCP_OAUTH_WORKOS_SUBDOMAIN=acme.authkit.app
# Better Auth: the auth URL, which is the issuer.
# MCP_OAUTH_BETTER_AUTH_URL=https://auth.example.com/api/auth
# Convex: the auth URL.
# MCP_OAUTH_CONVEX_AUTH_URL=https://example.convex.site
# 1 (or true) makes the server log why a token was refused (signature,
# issuer, audience, sub, exp) with the claims it checked, never the token; the
# 401's WWW-Authenticate header names the reason too.
# MCP_OAUTH_DEBUG=1
#
# Local playground: `docker compose --profile auth up -d` adds a Keycloak on
# host port 8480 (realm `miragon`, user demo/demo, seeded from
# playground/docker/keycloak/realm-miragon.json; admin console admin/admin).
# Point the server at it with the three lines below (`pnpm play --auth` sets
# them); MCP clients register with the IdP directly (Dynamic Client
# Registration: the seeded Trusted Hosts policy admits clients with loopback
# redirect URIs and rejects foreign hosts; it also trusts github.com, the
# client_uri the MCP Inspector names). The seeded realm stamps the
# required aud http://localhost:8400/mcp via the `mcp-resource` client scope
# (RFC 8707) and keeps the `sub` claim the provider requires via the seeded
# `basic` scope. Realm-file changes need `docker compose --profile auth up -d
# --force-recreate keycloak` to re-import.
# MCP_URL=http://localhost:8400
# MCP_OAUTH_KEYCLOAK_SERVER_URL=http://localhost:8480
# MCP_OAUTH_KEYCLOAK_REALM=miragon

# ── Optional thresholds ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The live verdict's thresholds (defaults 50 / 25), for engine_status and the
# cockpit overview alike: "critical" at >= CRITICAL_INCIDENTS open incidents in
# total, or when one cause reaches >= CRITICAL_CLUSTER_SIZE incidents.
# ENGINE_HEALTH_CRITICAL_INCIDENTS=50
# ENGINE_HEALTH_CRITICAL_CLUSTER_SIZE=25
